<Project>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       .github CONSUMES THE ORG-SHARED BUILD CONFIG IT AUTHORS (.github#609).

       This repo is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for dist/dotnet/Directory.Build.props — the file
       scripts/sync-build-config.sh distributes to sdd / rendering / governance / templates /
       game / audio. Until this file existed, .github was the one repo NOT covered by the thing
       it wrote: none of its own .NET projects consumed it, so ADR-0032's
       DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder never applied here and the F# SDK's library-packs copy
       of FSharp.Core kept leaking into restores. That is not theoretical — it red CI with
       NU1403 on the coordination engine's first push (#608), and each of those projects then
       had to set the property BY HAND to get itself green. Those hand-set copies are gone now:
       the config is inherited, from one place, exactly as it is in every receiver.

       WHY THIS IMPORTS THE ORG FILE RATHER THAN MIRRORING IT.

       Every other repo receives a byte-identical COPY, kept honest by the drift check
       (sync-build-config.sh in check mode), which is a required gate there. .github cannot be a
       plain receiver of that check, because the check covers all three managed files and one of
       them, .config/dotnet-tools.json, pins fs.gg.coord.cli FROM THE FEED. .github builds that
       tool from source and deliberately does NOT depend on the feed that ships it (see
       src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli/FS.GG.Coord.Cli.fsproj): a broken feed must never be able to stop the
       coordination tool being fixed. Adopting the manifest here would trade that property away.

       So a mirrored copy would have no gate keeping it fresh — and a STALE copy of a build
       config is worse than no copy at all, because it looks adopted. An import cannot drift by
       construction: it reads the live source of truth, in the repo that owns it. That is the
       stronger form of the same guarantee, and the honest meaning of "the authority repo is a
       consumer of its own contract".

       REPO-SPECIFIC OVERRIDES GO BELOW THE IMPORT, in this file. The org file's own
       Directory.Build.local.props hook resolves against ITS OWN directory
       ($(MSBuildThisFileDirectory) there is dist/dotnet/), so it is not the hook to reach for
       from here. MSBuild is last-definition-wins, so a property set after the import overrides
       the org default — which is the same escape hatch a receiver gets, just spelled locally.

       (Note for editors, inherited from the org file: an XML comment may not contain a double
       hyphen, so CLI flags are named above without their leading dashes.)
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)dist/dotnet/Directory.Build.props" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FS.GG.Kit, FS.GG.Drivers AND coord-engine (FS.GG.Coord.Cli) ARE A COHERENT SET (.github#2402).

       Three packages that ship from this repo used to carry three independent, hand-advanced
       <Version> literals — one per .csproj/.fsproj. They frequently ship the same behaviour change
       (.github#2135 landed as two separate hand bumps twenty minutes apart: commits f26da6ed /
       d48e1ec2) and nothing compared the three literals to each other, so a bump to one and not the
       others was representable and, twice, happened. This property is the fix: ONE scalar, declared
       once, that all three project files resolve their own <Version> from
       ($(FsggCoherentSetVersion) below), so the three cannot diverge from EACH OTHER by construction
       — a change to one moves this property, which moves all three.

       THIS DOES NOT MERGE THE THREE PACKAGES, their release cadence, or their publish workflows —
       that is a separate, larger, deliberately deferred change (see .github#2402's follow-up; a
       single shared release trigger is a maintainer sequencing decision on the fleet's sole
       coordination-engine distribution path, not a call made here). This property only removes the
       ability for their VERSION NUMBERS to drift from one another; each package still builds, packs
       and publishes through its own existing workflow, at whatever cadence that workflow runs, at
       this one shared version.

       STARTING VALUE. `0.50.0` — one MINOR above `max(FS.GG.Kit 0.49.0, FS.GG.Drivers 0.18.0,
       FS.GG.Coord.Cli 0.23.0)` = `0.49.0` as of 2026-08-11, the highest of the three prior
       independent versions, so adopting the shared scalar cannot make any member appear to
       downgrade. It is NOT `0.49.0` itself: `0.49.0` was already the newest FS.GG.Kit published on
       nuget.org at declaration time, and `check-kit-published-coherence.py`'s PR arm requires any PR
       that edits `src/FS.GG.Kit/FS.GG.Kit.csproj` — which adopting this property necessarily does —
       to declare a version STRICTLY GREATER than what is already published (verified live: the PR
       that first tried `0.49.0` reproduced exactly this red). `0.50.0` satisfies both constraints at
       once. See docs/registry/compatibility.md for the full migration note. Advancing this property
       is now the ONLY way to bump any of the three packages' <Version>; do not reintroduce an
       independent <Version> literal in any of their project files —
       scripts/check-coherent-set-version.py gates exactly that, with gate-inversion evidence in
       tests/coherent-set-version/.

       WHY THIS LIVES BELOW THE ORG IMPORT, NOT IN IT. The imported dist/dotnet/Directory.Build.props
       is byte-identical across every receiver (sync-build-config.sh's subject); it carries no
       <Version> property today and must not gain one on this repo's behalf — FsggCoherentSetVersion
       names packages that exist ONLY in .github (ADR-0039 §5: .github is a producer here, uniquely
       among receivers). This is exactly the "repo-specific override goes below the import" escape
       hatch the comment above already describes, used for the first time.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- 0.50.0 to 0.50.1 (.github#2451): a same-line patch bump. Required so check-kit-published
       coherence.py's PR arm sees a version STRICTLY GREATER than 0.50.0, already published on both
       feeds — this PR edits independent-review.md, a "kit:" source in registry/repos.yml. This PR
       also changes src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/Driver.fs and Review.fs, so coord-engine genuinely changes
       too; moving the whole coherent set together is honest rather than incidental, per the property
       group above's own rule (advancing this scalar is the ONLY way to bump any of the three). -->
  <!-- 0.50.3 to 0.50.4 (.github#2500): another same-line patch bump, same cause and shape as 0.50.2
       to 0.50.3 (3172d4ac). .github#2496 merged 182a2a62 after FS.GG.Kit 0.50.3 was packed at
       42c63af9, editing pnext-item/SKILL.md — a kit-packed source — so the published 0.50.3 kit
       drifted from canonical and check-kit-published-coherence reds on main. No engine source change;
       this bump exists solely to let the coherent set republish with current canonical content. -->
  <!-- 0.50.4 to 0.50.5 (.github#2504): same-line patch bump, same cause and shape as 0.50.3 to 0.50.4
       above — this PR edits pnext-item/SKILL.md, a kit-packed source, moving the live `delivery` call
       ahead of the `landable` wait so §6's merge order stays satisfiable now that `claim-generation`
       is a required status context (.github#1858). No engine source change; this bump exists solely to
       let FS.GG.Kit republish above the currently-published 0.50.4 with the corrected skill content. -->
  <!-- 0.50.5 to 0.50.6 (.github#2512): NOT the usual republish bump. 0.50.5 IS A PERMANENT
       TWO-OF-THREE SET and must not be adopted. The bump above moved this scalar and did NOT update
       FS.GG.Coord.Cli.fsproj's PackageReleaseNotes, which still opened "0.50.4" — so at release time
       check-engine-release-notes (.github#1762) correctly refused to publish the engine, AFTER
       FS.GG.Kit 0.50.5 and FS.GG.Drivers 0.50.5 had already published to the feeds. Tags are
       immutable and .github#1772's sibling-tag precondition requires all three tags at the SAME
       commit, so 0.50.5 cannot be completed from a corrected tree: correcting the notes needs a new
       commit, and that is a new version. This is exactly the 0.50.1 shape, recorded verbatim in
       registry/dependencies.yml. 0.50.6 is that corrected cut — notes and scalar moved TOGETHER,
       which is the whole point — and it is the version the fleet should adopt.

       WHY IT COULD HAPPEN AT ALL, AND WHAT NOW PREVENTS IT. A PR-time arm of that same checker
       already existed (.github/workflows/engine-release-notes.yml), but its `paths:` filter did not
       select THIS FILE — only the project file. A bump that lands solely here therefore ran none of
       it, and the gate first spoke at release time, which is after the irrevocable part. Both that
       filter and the checker's own PATHS_SUBJECT now name this file, and check-paths-coherence rule
       (c) plus tests/engine-release-notes/run.sh keep the two from drifting apart again. -->
  <!-- 0.50.6 to 0.50.7 (.github#2526): same-line patch bump, the ordinary republish shape — same cause
       as 0.50.3 to 0.50.4 and 0.50.4 to 0.50.5 above, NOT the two-of-three repair immediately above.
       This PR edits pnext-item/SKILL.md, a kit-packed source, correcting three prose claims that call
       `claim-generation` advisory-only when it has been a REQUIRED context since .github#1858. No
       engine source change and no behaviour change of any kind (AC4).

       WHY A NEW VERSION RATHER THAN RIDING 0.50.6. `kit-auto-publish` tags on merge to `main`, so
       0.50.6 was cut and published from .github#2512's merge commit 8de950c3 minutes before this
       branch merged `origin/main` — measured, not assumed: `git merge-base` in its ancestor-test form
       exits 1 for 5daf9126 against `kit/v0.50.6^{}`, and SKILL.md at that tag hashes e86617534873ba74
       and still contains
       "stays advisory in the engine's own rollup". The published FS.GG.Kit 0.50.6 is byte-faithful to
       its tagged tree; the tagged tree simply predates this fix. So the corrected skill body cannot
       reach receivers under 0.50.6, and republishing 0.50.6 is impossible (immutable tags). -->
  <!-- 0.50.7 to 0.51.0 (.github#2527): a MINOR bump, and the line change is the point. The three
       preceding cuts (0.50.5, 0.50.6, 0.50.7) were prose and release-notes corrections on the patch
       line. This one carries RECEIVER-VISIBLE BEHAVIOUR: the review protocol admits a case it
       previously refused, so `review` can return a different action for comment evidence a receiver
       already had, and the JSON render of `review` gains a `retiredChains` array.

       WHY NOT PATCH, given Protocol.fs does not move. The "no verb, flag, help entry, render name,
       result name or exit code moved" test the registry rows apply detects MOVED public surface; it
       does not detect ADDED behaviour, so it is necessary and not sufficient. The rule this cut
       follows is the one check-kit-published-coherence states itself: patch for a comment or doc
       edit, MINOR when the change is receiver-visible behaviour, a distinction no gate can draw for
       itself.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, NOT A FAULT: kit-auto-publish admits only a same-line patch bump
       (.github#2442), so it will refuse this candidate with `candidate-not-next-patch`. That refusal
       is the rail working as designed; a coherent-set version publishes through .github#2409's
       release path instead, from three sibling tags at ONE commit.

       NOTES AND SCALAR MOVE IN THIS SAME COMMIT, which is .github#2512's requirement and the whole
       reason 0.50.5 is a permanent two-of-three set. -->
  <!-- 0.51.0 to 0.51.1 (.github#2533): a same-line PATCH bump, and the reason it is a patch is the
       rule check-kit-published-coherence states about itself. That item's kit-visible change is
       PROSE: `.claude/skills/pnext-item/references/merge-and-release.md` gains the table of which
       post-merge acts are automated on merge into `main`, and the rule that a pre-act stop condition
       on an automated act belongs in a PRE-MERGE gate. No engine verb, flag, help entry, render
       name, result name or exit code moves, and no receiver-visible engine behaviour is added; the
       item's other half is a new CI gate arm, which ships in this repository rather than in any
       package.

       WHY THE BUMP IS REQUIRED AT ALL, and why it was not required when this branch opened.
       check-kit-published-coherence.py's PR arm demands a `<Version>` STRICTLY GREATER than the
       newest STABLE FS.GG.Kit on nuget.org whenever a pull request edits a `kit:` source. That was
       satisfied for free while 0.51.0 was tagged and unpublished: the tree was legitimately ahead of
       the feed and this change rode into the pending release. `FS.GG.Kit 0.51.0` then PUBLISHED
       during this item's work, the feed caught up with the tree, and the ride-along stopped being
       available. An evidence claim has to hold at the commit it ships with, not the commit it was
       measured on; re-reading the feed before pushing is what caught this one.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE ENTRY ABOVE: kit-auto-publish admits a
       same-line patch bump (.github#2442), so unlike the 0.51.0 minor it refused, it will ADMIT this
       candidate and cut all three sibling tags at the merge commit itself. Confirmed by running
       `scripts/kit-auto-publish.py` over the post-merge facts rather than inferred from #2442's
       prose. That makes the release an AUTOMATED act, so this item's post-merge obligation is to
       VERIFY the published bytes against canonical, never to perform the release by hand. That
       distinction is the whole subject of .github#2533.

       NOTES AND SCALAR MOVE IN THIS SAME COMMIT, for the reason the entry above records. -->
  <!-- 0.51.1 to 0.52.0 (.github#2549): a MINOR bump, and the reason is the criterion the entry
       immediately above states about itself. That one was a patch because "no engine verb, flag, help
       entry, render name, result name or exit code moves, and no receiver-visible engine behaviour is
       added". This change moves two of those and adds the third:

         * a new RENDER NAME on the `fsgg.coord.review/1` wire — `state: "acceptedAwaitingChecks"`;
         * a new RESULT NAME on the same wire — `action: "authorizeDelivery"`;
         * receiver-visible engine behaviour: a well-formed, host-accepted chain whose checks are not
           green now reports that new state with `stateErrors: null`, where it previously reported
           `malformedEvidence` with `["review checks are not green"]`. A consumer switching on `state`
           sees a value it has never seen, and — more to the point — a consumer that treated
           `malformedEvidence` as "this chain is broken" was being told something false on every
           ordinary landing.

       It also adds one OPTIONAL input key, `facts.repairAssertionGranted`, read additively: a snapshot
       that omits it parses exactly as before, which is why this is a minor and not a major.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE ENTRY ABOVE. `kit-auto-publish` admits only
       a same-line PATCH bump (.github#2442), so it will REFUSE this candidate with
       `candidate-not-next-patch` and cut nothing. That refusal is the rail working, not a fault. The
       release is therefore a MANUAL coherent-set publish through .github#2409's path, exactly as the
       0.51.0 minor was — which means this item's post-merge obligation is to PERFORM the release and
       then verify it, not merely to verify an automatic one. Measured, not inferred from #2442's prose:
       `scripts/kit-auto-publish.py` was run over this candidate's facts and its refusal recorded on the
       pull request, on the same discipline the entry above used.

       A two-of-three set is permanent and uncompletable from a corrected tree — that is how 0.50.1 and
       0.50.5 became permanent gaps — so all three sibling tags publish at the SAME merge commit.

       NOTES AND SCALAR MOVE IN THIS SAME COMMIT, for the reason the entries above record.

       0.55.0 -> 0.56.0 is a MINOR. .github#2561 changes receiver-visible done-stamp behaviour for
       large closure and project-item sets: four previously bare GraphQL windows now refuse an
       incomplete read instead of silently deriving red, green, or NoStatus from a hidden tail.
       This new refusal boundary is observable coordination behaviour, so a patch would understate
       the change and the coherent set requires a real release.

       0.56.0 -> 0.57.0 is a MINOR. .github#2587 changes receiver-visible packed pnext-item review
       guidance: issue and pull-request body path/count/suite claims now require repository/revision
       and command/check provenance before they can serve as acceptance evidence. This is a new
       reviewer protocol boundary, not a correction to an existing description; the engine and
       drivers move only because all three packages are one coherent set.

       0.58.0 -> 0.58.1 is a PATCH, and .github#2571 is the item. The packed change is one correction
       to `pnext-item`'s `merge-and-release.md`: row 1 read `kit-auto-publish.yml`'s TRIGGER and stated
       its ACT, so it told every worker that the coherent-set release is something the merge always
       performs. It is not — `kit-auto-publish.py` admits only a same-line patch bump (.github#2442) and
       terminally refuses a minor, which every coherent-set release is. That is a FALSE CLAUSE removed
       from an existing description, not a new receiver protocol boundary, which is the distinction the
       0.56.0 and 0.57.0 entries above drew when they took minors. The behaviour change proper lives in
       `scripts/check-kit-published-coherence.py`, a CI gate this repository runs and the kit does not
       pack (`registry/repos.yml`'s `kit:` block does not name it), so no receiver's behaviour moves
       with it. .github#2533 — the same shape, a CI-gate change plus a section added to this same
       document — took a patch at 18af7595 for the same reason.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE 0.52.0 ENTRY ABOVE: 0.58.1 IS the next patch
       above both feed frontiers, so `kit-auto-publish` will cut all three sibling tags at the merge
       commit and the release is AUTOMATIC. This item's post-merge obligation is therefore to VERIFY
       that release against canonical, never to perform it — which is `merge-and-release.md`'s own
       patch-line rule, the item self-applying rather than exempting itself. Measured, not inferred:
       the frontier was read from nuget.org and `scripts/kit-auto-publish.py` was run over this
       candidate's facts, on the same discipline the entries above used. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <FsggCoherentSetVersion>0.58.1</FsggCoherentSetVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
